namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

/*! 
\page rp_BinaryStructureFactor_page BinaryStructureFactor

This analyzer computes the structure factor for any "binary" system, 
with only two types of monomer. By default, it computes and outputs 
only the spherical average of the structure factor, for which each
value is an averaged over all wavevectors in a set (or "bunch") of
wavevectors of equal magnitude. This class can also optionally 
output structure factors for all individual wavevectors.

\see BinaryStructureFactor (class API)

\section rp_BinaryStructureFactor_formula_sec Theory

This class computes the structure factor \f$ S({\bf q}) \f$ for an 
incompressible system containing two monomer types, for all values
of the wavevector \f$ {\bf q} \f$. Let the two monomer types be denoted
by subscripts 0 and 1. The structure factor computed here is defined 
as
\f[
  S(\mathbf{q}) 
  = \frac{1}{4V}\langle |\hat{C}_{-}({\bf q})|^{2} \rangle \quad,
\f]
where \f$ V \f$ is total system volume, and 
\f[
  \hat{C}_{-}({\bf q}) \equiv \int \! d{\bf r} 
                       \; e^{-i{\bf q}\cdot {\bf r}}
                       [ c_{0}({\bf r}) - c_{1}({\bf r}) ] 
  \quad.
\f]
Here, \f$ c_{i}({\bf r}) \f$ is the local concentration of monomers of 
type i, for i = 0 and i =1.
In a field-theoretic simulation of an AB system, this is computed using 
the identity
\f[
  S(\mathbf{q}) = 
  \frac{1}{v^2\chi^2} \frac{1}{V}
  \left\langle |\hat{W}_{-}(\mathbf {q})|^{2} \right\rangle
   - \frac{1}{2v \chi} 
\f]
where \f$ v \f$ is monomer volume, \f$ \chi \f$ is the bare Flory-Huggins 
parameter \f$ \chi = \chi_{12} - (\chi_{11} + \chi_{22})/2  \f$, and
\f[
  \hat{W}_{-}({\bf q}) = \int \! d{\bf r} 
  \; e^{-i{\bf q}\cdot {\bf r}} 
  [ w_{0}({\bf r}) - w_{1}({\bf r})]/2 
\f]
is the Fourier transform of the exchange field 
\f$ W_{-}({\bf r}) = [ w_{0}({\bf r}) - w_{1}({\bf r})]/2 \f$.

<em> Technical note </em>:
The Fourier transform \f$ \hat{W}_{-}({\bf q}) \f$ that is defined above 
as a spatial integral is approximated internally by the product of the 
system volume \f$ V \f$ and the normalized discrete Fourier transform 
(DFT) of \f$ W_{-}({\bf r}) \f$ that is computed by either 
Prdc::Cpu::FFT::forwardTransform or
Prdc::Cuda::FFT::forwardTransform .

\section rp_BinaryStructureFactor_parameter_sec Parameter file

Parameter file format:
\code
BinaryStructureFactor{
  interval*          int   (1 by default)
  outputFileName     string
  writeWaveData*     bool  (false/0 by default)
}
\endcode
Meanings of the parameters:
<table>
  <tr>
    <td> <b> Label </b>  </td>
    <td> <b> Description </b>  </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td> interval* </td>
    <td> number of steps between data samples </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
     <td> outputFileName </td>
     <td> base name of output files </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
     <td> writeWaveData* </td>
     <td> Should \f$ S({\bf q})\f$ be reported for individual waves? </td>
  </tr>
</table>
Note that the writeWaveData parameter is optional, and set to false 
(0) by default. 

\section rp_BinaryStructureFactor_output_sec Output Files

This class does not output any data during the course of a simulation
or trajectory file analysis, but instead outputs accumulated results 
at the end.

<b> Spherically-averaged structure factor </b>:
At the end of the simulation, this class outputs a file containing 
spherically-averaged values of \f$ S({\bf q}) \f$, averaged over time 
and over groups of wavevectors of the same magnitude. If writeWaveData 
is false (0. the default), then the name of this file is given by the 
outputFileName string parameter. If writeWaveData is true (1), then the 
name of this file is obtained by adding a suffix "_ave" to the end of 
the outputFileName string. In either case, the resulting file contains
two columns of numbers, in which the first column contains an ordered 
list of values of the wavenumber \f$ q = |{\bf q}|\f$, and the second 
column contains a corresponding list of values of the spherically 
averaged structure factor \f$ S(q) \f$. 

<b> Structure factor for individual wavevectors </b>:
If writeWaveData is true, then, at the end of the simulation, this class 
also outputs a second file containing values of S(q) for individual 
wavevectors.  The name of this file is obtained by appending a suffix 
"_wave" to the outputFileName string parameter.  The resulting file
contain \f$ D + 1 \f$ columns of data, where \f$ D \f$ is the dimension
of space. The first D columns contain integer indices for individual 
wavevectors in a basis of reciprocal lattice basis vectors, using a 
standard DFT grid representation in which all indices are non-negative.  
The last column contains time averaged values of \f$ S({\bf q}) \f$ 
for each wavevector.

*/

}
}
